<comment datatype='string'>The &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operon, characterized from enteric bacteria such as &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-28901&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Salmonella enterica&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, encodes enzymes required for the catabolism of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHANOL-AMINE&quot;&gt;ethanolamine&lt;/a&gt; and its use as a carbon, nitrogen, and energy source [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/1090845&quot;&gt;Chang75b&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/784902&quot;&gt;Scarlett76&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/6374020&quot;&gt;Jones84b&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/7868611&quot;&gt;Stojiljkovic95a&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
In these organisms the pathway occur within the lumen of a bacterial microcompartment (MPC) known as the &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; metabolosome, a capsid-like shell composed of multiple proteins that is analogous to the carboxysome of cyanobacteria [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16585748&quot;&gt;Penrod06&lt;/a&gt;]. A similar compartment is used by these organsisms for the catabolism of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=PROPANE-1-2-DIOL&quot;&gt;(&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-propane-1,2-diol&lt;/a&gt; (see &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-7013&quot;&gt;(&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-propane-1,2-diol degradation&lt;/a&gt;). The metabolosome serves several functions - it protects the cells from the toxicity of the acetaldehyde intermediate [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16291677&quot;&gt;Brinsmade05&lt;/a&gt;]; it prevent carbon loss from volatilization of acetaldehyde [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16585748&quot;&gt;Penrod06&lt;/a&gt;]; and it makes it easier to recycle cofactors used in ethanolamine catabolism [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/23585538&quot;&gt;Huseby13&lt;/a&gt;]. The &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operon, whose expression is induced by the presence of ethanolamine and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ADENOSYLCOBALAMIN&quot;&gt;adenosylcobalamin&lt;/a&gt;, encodes all functions needed for the assembly and function of the metabolosome, as well as the enzymes of the3 pathway [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10464203&quot;&gt;Kofoid99&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/23920494&quot;&gt;Held13&lt;/a&gt;]. Full &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operons have been found in Enterobacteriaceae, Firmicutes, Nocardioides sp., and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-851&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Fusobacterium nucleatum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. It should be noted that many other organisms contain a much shorter operon that often includes only the genes encoding the first enzyme of the pathway, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-4.3.1.7&quot;&gt;EC 4.3.1.7, ethanolamine ammonia-lyase&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/19783625&quot;&gt;Tsoy09&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
In the intestine, &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHANOL-AMINE&quot;&gt;ethanolamine&lt;/a&gt; is derived from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=L-1-PHOSPHATIDYL-ETHANOLAMINE&quot; title=&quot;Phosphatidylethanolamines are a class of &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Phosphoglycerides''&gt;phosphoglycerolipids&lt;/a&gt; found in biological membranes. They are synthesized by the addition of an ethanolamine group from CDP-ethanolamine to &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Diacylglycerides''&gt;diglycerides&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;Phosphatidylethanolamines are found in all living cells, composing 25% of all phospholipids.&quot;&gt;phosphatidylethanolamine&lt;/a&gt;, a major component of eukaryotic membranes [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4888138&quot;&gt;Fung69&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/6304089&quot;&gt;Larson83&lt;/a&gt;]. Use of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHANOL-AMINE&quot;&gt;ethanolamine&lt;/a&gt; as a carbon or nitrogen source requires &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ADENOSYLCOBALAMIN&quot;&gt;adenosylcobalamin&lt;/a&gt;, which serves as the cofactor of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-4.3.1.7&quot;&gt;EC 4.3.1.7, ethanolamine ammonia-lyase&lt;/a&gt; and a coinducer (with ethanolamine) of the &lt;i&gt;eut&lt;/i&gt; operon transcription. While &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, which is not capable of producing &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ADENOSYLCOBALAMIN&quot;&gt;adenosylcobalamin&lt;/a&gt; &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt;, requires an external source of the cofactor, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-28901&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Salmonella enterica&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; can produce it under anaerobic conditions. It has been shown that one of the genes of the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-28901&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Salmonella enterica&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; operon, &lt;i&gt;eutT&lt;/i&gt;, encodes &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.5.1.17&quot;&gt;EC 2.5.1.17, corrinoid adenosyltransferase&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/15516577&quot;&gt;Sheppard04&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The operon also includes the &lt;i&gt;eutE&lt;/i&gt; gene, which is assumed to encode &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-1.2.1.10&quot;&gt;EC 1.2.1.10, acetaldehyde dehydrogenase (acetylating)&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G7288&quot;&gt;eutD&lt;/a&gt;, which encodes the next enzyme in the pathway, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.3.1.8&quot;&gt;EC 2.3.1.8, phosphate acetyltransferase&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/14996820&quot;&gt;Brinsmade04&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
</comment>
